<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Colonia del Sacramento</h1><p class="st-meta">1680 CE – 1777 CE · capital Colonia del Sacramento</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Portuguese outpost on the River Plate opposite Buenos Aires that changed hands repeatedly as Spain and Portugal fought for the estuary.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1680 the Portuguese governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-lobo">Manuel Lobo</a> to found Colônia do Santíssimo Sacramento on the northern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">River Plate</a>, directly across from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> captured it within months, but a treaty of 1681 returned it to Portugal.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Colonia was a fortified town and a base for smuggling: Portuguese and British goods and enslaved Africans flowed into the Spanish territories in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> and hides. Its presence provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1724. Spanish forces besieged it in 1704, 1735 and 1762. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1750">Treaty of Madrid</a> of 1750 Portugal agreed to exchange it for the Seven Missions, but the deal collapsed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-cevallos">Pedro de Cevallos</a> captured Colonia for the last time in 1777, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-treaty-of-san-ildefonso">Treaty of San Ildefonso</a> that year confirmed Spanish possession. It became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Uruguay</a>. Its historic quarter, with Portuguese and Spanish streets, became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1995.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colonia-del-sacramento.jpg" alt="Colonia del Sacramento" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: JGHowes · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_del_Sanct%C3%ADsimo_Sacramento.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1680 CE – 1777 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Colonia del Sacramento</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuel Lobo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colônia do Sacramento, Nova Colônia do Santíssimo Sacramento</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1728&amp;focus=colonia-del-sacramento" aria-label="Colonia del Sacramento on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3211 3425 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colonia del Sacramento lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3531 3681L4773 1044M3531 3681L4910 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3531 3681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3531 3681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1728&amp;focus=colonia-del-sacramento">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

